## Secrets handling (acceptance criterion 3)

```json
// envs/prod/secrets.tfvars.json  → encrypted with SOPS, never committed plaintext
{ "db_password": "..." }
```

Deployment:

```bash
cd envs/prod
sops -d secrets.tfvars.json > /tmp/secrets.tfvars.json
terraform init
terraform apply -var-file=terraform.tfvars -var-file=/tmp/secrets.tfvars.json
rm /tmp/secrets.tfvars.json
```

Or via workspaces instead of directories:

```bash
terraform workspace new dev
terraform workspace new prod
terraform apply -var-file=prod.tfvars
```

---

## Acceptance criteria mapping

| # | How met |
|---|---|
| 1 | Versioning enabled, SSE (AES256/KMS per env), full public access block |
| 2 | `deletion_protection = true`, `backup_retention_period >= 7`, `publicly_accessible = false` |
| 3 | DB password via SOPS-encrypted tfvars, marked `sensitive` |
| 4 | OIDC-trust role scoped to one service account; explicit S3 actions, no wildcards (except resource suffix, which is unavoidable for object ops) |
| 7 | Separate state per env (backend keys / directories); prod changes can't touch dev |

Note: EKS/ALB/CloudFront pieces are listed in the spec but your task scoped the module to bucket + RDS + IAM — the RDS SG is wired to accept the EKS node SG, and the IAM role assumes the cluster's OIDC provider exists.
